  "text": "A model wants numbers, not lists of words. So this unit turns text into features: rows of numbers a program can learn from. But first, a choice. The cleaning steps are a menu, not a recipe. You'll nearly always tokenize. After that, every step is a question. Lowercase? Drop stopwords? Stem or lemmatize? Add n-grams? And each one has the same test. Does what this step throws away matter to your task? If it's noise, drop it. If it's signal, keep it.\n\nTake one review. The plot was not good, but the acting was brilliant. A topic pipeline drops every stopword. The words not and but are both on NLTK's list, so what's left is plot, good, acting, brilliant. That's fine for what the review is about. But read it for feeling, and it now just says good. A sentiment pipeline keeps the negations, and adds word pairs, so not good survives as a unit.\n\nEvery task chooses differently. Search wants stems. Topics want real words, so lemmas. Sentiment keeps its stopwords. And to tell who wrote a text, the stopwords are the signal itself. So don't guess. Change one step at a time, and measure on your own task. That's called an ablation. A safe default: tokenize, lowercase, and keep the stopwords until a measurement says to drop them. With the tokens chosen, the next job is to count them.",
